ACLU * ACLU * ACLU * ACLU * ACLU * ACLU * ACLU * ACLU * ACLU 
 NEWS RELEASE * NEWS RELEASE * NEWS RELEASE * NEWS RELEASE  
 
Montana School Board Learns the Costs of Censorship; 
ACLU Reaches $38,000 Settlement on Behalf of Fired Library Aide 
 
For IMMEDIATE RELEASE 
February 3, 1995 
 
	A public school library aide in Montana who was fired for sharing 
information and her own books with students has won her case with the help 
of the American Civil Liberties Union.  
 
	Debbie Denzer sued the school board and the principal who fired 
her after she loaned two books about women's history and witchcraft to two 
7th grade girls. This week she signed a $38,000 settlement of her claims 
against the West Valley School District in Kalispell, Montana.  
 
	"I'm relieved and gratified that the school board has finally 
realized its error and the harm it has caused me," Denzer said. "It wasn't 
an easy choice for me to confront the powers that be and challenge the 
small-minded censorship that has deprived me of the work I love -- showing 
kids the joys of reading and learning. I hope that other library workers 
will benefit and that this case will help school boards resist the urge to 
censor," Denzer added.  
 
	Denzer's vindication has repercussions beyond rural Montana, 
according to Marjorie Heins, Director of the national ACLU's Arts 
Censorship Project, who represented Denzer as co-counsel with Montana ACLU 
cooperating attorney Joan Jonkel.  
 
	"Debbie Denzer was punished because she loved books and tried to 
share her enthusiasm for learning with students," Heins said. 
"Unfortunately, examples of censorship, including punishment of school 
staff for providing reading materials, have reached epidemic levels 
throughout America," Heins added.  "Ideological groups, often 
fundamentalist in orientation, are demanding that books whose subjects or 
viewpoints displease them be removed from curriculum reading lists and 
library shelves."  
 
	 According to the ACLU's Arts Censorship Project, the most frequent 
targets of censorship are sexual topics, particularly homosexuality, 
violent themes, and, as in Debbie Denzer's case, feminism, witchcraft, or 
the occult. From a book-burning of a gay-themed book in a Kansas town to 
the removal of a book on Voodoo in Louisiana to attempts by a Georgia 
school board to ban all "sexually explicit" books, the ACLU's Heins said 
there have been an alarming number of attempts to limit the content of 
materials available in the schools.  
 
	Denzer's problems began in December 1993 when two 7th grade girls 
came into the library requesting her help with a research project on 
witchcraft in the Middle Ages.  The library contained nothing pertinent 
beyond encyclopedia entries, so Denzer promised to search her home 
bookshelves, as she had in the past when the school library proved 
inadequate for students' or teachers' needs.  At home, she found two books 
that answered the girls' request: Not in God's Image: Women in History 
From the Greeks to the Victorians by Julia O'Faolain and Lauro Matines (a 
text of primary sources on the oppression of women in history); and The 
Devil and All His Works, by Dennis Wheatley, a critically acclaimed 
American Heritage survey of occultism.  
 
	The day after Denzer made the loan, however, the parents of one of 
the girls visited both Denzer and the principal of West Valley School, 
Frank deKort, to complain about the books.  The parents indicated that the 
texts' "satanist" and "feminist" content offended their religious beliefs, 
even though they were aware that practices documented in the books, such 
as wife-beating, do occur.  Although the parents said they did not want to 
see Denzer lose her job, deKort suspended her immediately, and recommended 
her dismissal.  The school board obliged at a meeting a few weeks later.  
 
	School Board members at a January 1994 meeting derided the two 
books for "vulgarity," focussing on a few lurid images; no member had 
actually read more than brief excerpts from either text.  One board member 
asserted that the books were "derogatory toward women" and robbed the 
girls of their "innocence."  Denzer was fired even though she had never 
been notified that she was expected to withhold books from students based 
on "vulgarity," feminism or any other potentially controversial content.  
 
	Denzer promptly filed an administrative appeal with the county 
superintendent, who ruled in June, after a hearing, that the school board 
had lacked any basis under state law to fire Denzer.  While the board's 
appeal of that administrative decision was pending, Denzer, represented by 
the Montana ACLU and the national ACLU's Arts Censorship Project, filed 
suit in federal court for violation of her, and the students', First 
Amendment and Due Process rights.  
 
	After several months of negotiations, lawyers for Denzer and the 
school board worked out the $38,000 settlement of the federal case.  Of 
the settlement, $30,000 will compensate Denzer for back pay, lost job 
opportunities, and emotional distress; $8,000 represents litigation costs 
and attorney's fees to the ACLU and Montana ACLU cooperating attorney Joan 
Jonkel. In addition, all references to the unlawful firing will be removed 
from Denzer's personnel file.  
 
	Scott Crichton, Executive Director of the ACLU of Montana, said: 
"Our public schools are under tremendous pressure to knuckle under to 
narrow political and religious ideologies that want to rob kids of what 
education is all about -- reading about new subjects; discovering new 
ideas; inquiring and exploring."  
 
	"The fact that Debbie Denzer fought back and won a substantial 
settlement for violation of her rights, should send a message to school 
boards that suppressing books because they disagree with the ideas they 
contain is not only wrongheaded but unconstitutional," Crichton said.  
