From caf-talk Caf Feb  7 14:26:28 1995
From: torkeld@aol.com (TorkelD)
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject: Freedom of Speech Is About To Become Irrelevant
Date: 7 Feb 1995 14:07:04 -0500
Message-ID: <3h8gco$3f3@newsbf02.news.aol.com>

I'll declare it up front -- I'm a big fan of the Constitution and the Bill
of Rights, every blessed, slightly imperfect, inspired word.  Freedom of
speech is one of America's founding principles, worth defending with blood
or ink (or electronic impulse, in this case).  BUT --

If the House passes "The Exclusionary Rule Reform Act of 1995" and the
Senate passes its "Violent Crime Control and Law Enforcement Improvement
Act of 1995," freedom of speech will be immaterial.  Both bills gut the
4th Amendment by allowing warrantless searches, and allow those
responsible to craft their "reasonable cause" after the fact.  The claim
is that the 4th amendment protects criminals, and it does.  It's hard to
write a law which protects the innocent but not the guilty, since we can't
always know which is which (criminals don't glow in the dark).  Freedom of
speech doesn't mean much if authority can enter your home when it pleases
and look for something, anything, with which to charge you.  I hope that
those currently in power remember they won't always hold the reigns, and
even if their successors belong to the same clubs, they may not interpret
their causes the same way - so today's wielder of power is tomorrow's
suspected subversive.  This should be something conservatives and liberals
can agree on -- whether your cause is Keeping Government Off Our Backs or
Protecting Civil Rights, this Amendment is for you.  If we don't make our
voices heard, we'll deserve what we get when this is done.  --  TorkelD 

