usually involves investing in life insurance or risky companies. What in 
practise seems to hgappen is that the money you would have paid in tax is 
saved, sure, but you donlt get it - it goes to the professional in fees. In 
the case of life insurance, the first few years premiums go to the 
professional, so you probably don't notice it unless your circumstances 
change and you encash the policy early. In the case of investment (BES etc) 
the fees are paid by the company, so again the investor doesn't notice them 
... until the company turns in bad results or goes bust because it could 
never support the professional fees on start-up.

A minimalist government could collect taxes in a painless way, probably by 
selling services at a profit or even such means as organising lotteries.

